tests/ir/transpose.sky:3:8: note: [LA-TRANSPOSE-001] `Aᵀ @ A` is rewritten to `matmul[ta](A, A)`
  |
3 | gram = Aᵀ @ A
  |        ^^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `transposes` pass
  = note: because the transposed operand is a matrix: `matmul[ta]` reads it transposed, so the 2·3 element copy that `transpose` makes is never made
  = note: because exact: `matmul[ta]` is defined as the transpose followed by the product, so every bit and every error is the same; a kernel may read the operand with swapped strides
  = note: because the flops are unchanged: 36

tests/ir/transpose.sky:3:8: note: [LA-GRAM-001] `Aᵀ @ A` is rewritten to `matmul[ta,alg=syrk](A, A)`
  |
3 | gram = Aᵀ @ A
  |        ^^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `structure` pass
  = note: because A is multiplied by its own transpose: entries (i, j) and (j, i) sum the same products (`aₚᵢ·aₚⱼ` and `aₚⱼ·aₚᵢ`, and multiplication commutes) in the same order
  = note: because one triangle is computed and mirrored: 24 flops instead of 36
  = note: because exact: the result is bit for bit the general product's, and bitwise symmetric (its type gains the Symmetric tag)

tests/ir/transpose.sky:4:8: note: [GVN-001] `transpose(A)` at line 4 reuses the value computed at line 3
  |
4 | same = transpose(A)
  |        ^^^^^^^^^^^^
3 | gram = Aᵀ @ A
  |        -- computed here first
  = note: applied by the `gvn` pass
  = note: because the earlier `transpose` has the same operands and runs first on every path to this one, so both compute the same value
